::GEO_U06_L05_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L05_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.14<br><br>Question:<br>Which formula gives the volume of a rectangular prism? {
=A. V = lwh#A rectangular prism volume is length times width times height.
~B. V = 2lw + 2lh + 2wh#This is surface area.
~C. V = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>Bh#This is for a pyramid or cone.
~D. V = &pi;r&sup2;h#This is cylinder volume.
}

::GEO_U06_L05_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L05_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.14<br><br>Question:<br>A pyramid has base area 30 and height 9. What is its volume? {
~A. 270 cubic units#This omits the one-third factor.
=B. 90 cubic units#Pyramid volume is <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>Bh, so <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>(30)(9)=90.
~C. 39 cubic units#This adds base area and height.
~D. 810 cubic units#This multiplies by 3 instead of dividing by 3.
}

::GEO_U06_L05_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L05_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.14<br><br>Question:<br>Which formula gives the volume of a sphere? {
~A. 4&pi;r&sup2;#This is sphere surface area.
~B. &pi;r&sup2;h#This is cylinder volume.
=C. <sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>&pi;r&sup3;#Sphere volume is <sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>&pi;r&sup3;.
~D. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>&pi;r&sup2;h#This is cone volume.
}

::GEO_U06_L05_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L05_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.14<br><br>Question:<br>A cylinder has radius 5 and height 2. What is its volume? {
~A. 10&pi; cubic units#This multiplies radius and height but does not square the radius.
~B. 20&pi; cubic units#This does not use r&sup2; correctly.
~C. 25&pi; cubic units#This finds base area only.
=D. 50&pi; cubic units#Cylinder volume is &pi;r&sup2;h = &pi;(5)&sup2;(2)=50&pi;.
}

::GEO_U06_L05_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L05_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.14<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement about volume units is correct? {
=A. Volume uses cubic units because it measures three-dimensional space#Volume measures length, width, and height effects.
~B. Volume uses square units because solids have surfaces#Square units describe surface area, not volume.
~C. Volume uses degrees because solids have angles#Degrees measure angles.
~D. Volume has no units#A real measurement needs appropriate units.
}

